---
import TestPage from '../../../components/us/TestPage.astro';
import TestCite from '../../../components/us/TestCite.astro';
import ReadingGuide from '../../../components/ReadingGuide.astro';
import { S } from '../../../lib/us/tests';

const src = {
  bpi: S('combustionSafety', 'BPI - Standards'),
  bpi1200: S('combustionSafety', 'BPI-1200-S-2017 Standard Practice'),
  caz: S('combustionSafety', 'Combustion Appliance Zone'),
  enclosure: S('combustionSafety', 'Enclosure Leakage Test'),
  cpsc: S('carbonMonoxide', 'Questions and Answers'),
};

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'When does a home need a combustion safety test?',
    a: 'DOE’s Building America Solution Center calls for combustion appliance zone (CAZ) testing in homes with natural-draft furnaces, boilers or water heaters, especially during air sealing, when it should be done at the end of each workday.',
    src: [src.caz],
  },
  {
    q: 'My appliances are direct-vent. Is the test still useful?',
    a: 'The standard test still measures carbon monoxide on direct-vent and vent-free equipment and on gas ovens. The spillage part applies to natural-draft and Category I appliances.',
    src: [src.bpi1200],
  },
  {
    q: 'What CO level is dangerous during an audit?',
    a: 'BPI-1200 sets ambient CO action levels: 9 to 35 ppm, advise checking sources and ventilating; 36 to 69 ppm, ventilate and shut off sources; 70 ppm or more, stop, evacuate and call emergency services from outside.',
    src: [src.bpi1200],
  },
  {
    q: 'Is this the same as having a CO alarm?',
    a: 'No. The test checks how your appliances vent under worst-case conditions on the day of the audit. CPSC recommends CO alarms on every level of the home and outside sleeping areas as well.',
    src: [src.cpsc],
  },
  {
    q: 'What does a combustion safety test cost?',
    a: 'No official source publishes a price, so we don’t quote one.',
  },
];

const sources = [src.bpi, src.bpi1200, src.caz, src.enclosure, src.cpsc];
---

<TestPage
  slug="combustion-safety"
  title="Combustion safety testing: BPI’s CO action levels and the worst-case draft test | AuditYourHome"
  description="Auditors test fuel-burning appliances for spillage and carbon monoxide under worst-case conditions. What BPI-1200 requires, what CO readings mean and when DOE calls for it."
  heroTitle="Combustion safety test: the check that belongs with air sealing"
  glance={[
    { k: 'Standard', v: 'ANSI/BPI-1200-S-2017', src: src.bpi, srcLabel: 'BPI' },
    { k: 'Stop and evacuate at', v: '70 ppm CO or more', src: src.bpi1200, srcLabel: 'BPI-1200' },
    { k: 'Gas check first', v: 'Below 10% of the LEL', src: src.bpi1200, srcLabel: 'BPI-1200' },
    { k: 'Typical cost', v: 'Part of the audit' },
  ]}
  faqs={faqs}
  sources={sources}
  leadHeading="Air sealing a home with gas, oil or propane appliances?"
  leadSub="Email us your ZIP code and what you’re planning. A person reads every message and replies with certified auditors who run combustion safety tests in your area."
>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    A combustion safety test checks whether furnaces, boilers and water heaters vent properly when the
    space around them is under the most suction the house can create, and measures carbon monoxide
    along the way. DOE’s Building America Solution Center calls for it in homes with natural-draft
    appliances, especially during air sealing.
  </Fragment>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="how-h">
    <h2 id="how-h">What the test involves</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <h3>Safety checks first</h3>
        <p>Before testing, the auditor confirms combustible gas is below 10% of the lower explosive limit and carbon monoxide in the air is below 70 ppm.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Set up the worst case</h3>
        <p>The auditor creates the greatest depressurization achievable in the combustion appliance zone (CAZ) for the conditions.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Check spillage and CO on natural-draft appliances</h3>
        <p>Spillage and CO are measured on natural-draft appliances (barometric draft control) and Category I appliances (draft hood or natural-draft vent).</p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Measure CO on the rest</h3>
        <p>CO is also measured on gas ovens and on direct-vent or vent-free equipment.</p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <TestCite src={src.bpi1200} labels={['ANSI/BPI-1200-S-2017']} />
  </section>

  <section class="sec" aria-labelledby="co-h">
    <h2 id="co-h">What the CO readings mean</h2>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table class="stack datatable">
        <caption>BPI-1200 ambient carbon monoxide action levels</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">CO in the air</th><th scope="col">What the auditor does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>9 to 35 ppm</td><td data-label="What the auditor does">Advises checking the sources and ventilating</td></tr>
          <tr><td>36 to 69 ppm</td><td data-label="What the auditor does">Ventilates and shuts off the sources</td></tr>
          <tr><td>70 ppm or more</td><td data-label="What the auditor does"><strong>Stops work, evacuates and calls emergency services from outside</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <TestCite src={src.bpi1200} labels={['ANSI/BPI-1200-S-2017']} />
  </section>

  <section class="sec prose" aria-labelledby="std-h">
    <h2 id="std-h">The standards behind it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>ANSI/BPI-1200-S-2017</strong>, Standard Practice for Basic Analysis of Buildings, is still listed on BPI’s standards page. Its Section 7 adopts the combustion appliance inspection requirements of BPI-1100 Section 7 and adds to them.</li>
      <li><strong>ANSI/BPI-1100-T-2023</strong>, the Home Energy Auditing Standard, is the current BPI-1100.</li>
      <li>DOE’s Building America Solution Center cites <strong>NFPA 54</strong> for combustion air.</li>
      <li>For ENERGY STAR homes that keep non-direct-vent appliances in climate zones 1–3, it cites RESNET Section 805 and ANSI/ACCA 12 QH-2014 Appendix A (a CO test and a CAZ depressurization test).</li>
    </ul>
    <TestCite src={[src.bpi, src.caz]} labels={['BPI standards', 'DOE BASC, CAZ testing']} />
  </section>

  <section class="sec prose" aria-labelledby="get-h">
    <h2 id="get-h">How to get it done</h2>
    <p>
      Ask auditors whether their assessment follows BPI-1200, which includes the combustion appliance
      inspection, and whether they’ll test at the end of each day of air sealing, as DOE describes.
      What it costs: no official source publishes a price for the test on its own, so we don’t quote one. In a home with
      gas, oil or propane appliances it belongs in any audit that includes air sealing, so ask for it in the audit quote rather
      than as an extra.
    </p>
    <ul class="linkrow">
      <li><a href="/us/find/">Find a certified auditor</a></li>
      <li><a href={src.bpi.href} rel="noopener">BPI standards&nbsp;↗</a></li>
    </ul>
    <TestCite src={[src.bpi, src.caz]} labels={['BPI standards', 'DOE BASC, CAZ testing']} />
  </section>

  <ReadingGuide country="us" test="combustion-safety" />

  <section class="sec prose" aria-labelledby="fix-h">
    <h2 id="fix-h">If an appliance fails</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>At 70 ppm or more in the air, everyone leaves and emergency services are called from outside, per BPI-1200.</li>
      <li>If an appliance spills or produces high CO, have it serviced or replaced by a qualified technician and retested before more air sealing is done.</li>
      <li>Make sure CO alarms are on every level and outside sleeping areas, as CPSC recommends. See the <a href="/us/tests/carbon-monoxide/">carbon monoxide guide</a>.</li>
    </ul>
    <TestCite src={[src.bpi1200, src.cpsc]} labels={['ANSI/BPI-1200-S-2017', 'CPSC CO questions and answers']} />
  </section>
</TestPage>
